@contents

@chapter Introduction

QuickJS (version @value{VERSION}) is a small and embeddable Javascript
engine. It supports most of the ES2025 specification
@footnote{@url{https://tc39.es/ecma262/2025 }}.

@section Main Features

@itemize

@item Small and easily embeddable: just a few C files, no external dependency, 210 KiB of x86 code for a simple ``hello world'' program.

@item Fast interpreter with very low startup time: runs the tests of the ECMAScript Test Suite@footnote{@url{https://github.com/tc39/test262}} in less than 2 minutes on a single core of a desktop PC. The complete life cycle of a runtime instance completes in less than 300 microseconds.

@item Almost complete ES2025 support.

@item Passes nearly 100% of the ECMAScript Test Suite tests when selecting the ES2025 features.

@item Compile Javascript sources to executables with no external dependency.

@item Garbage collection using reference counting (to reduce memory usage and have deterministic behavior) with cycle removal.

@item Command line interpreter with contextual colorization and completion implemented in Javascript.

@item Small built-in standard library with C library wrappers.

@end itemize

@chapter Usage

@section Installation

A Makefile is provided to compile the engine on Linux or MacOS/X.  A
preliminary Windows support is available thru cross compilation on a
Linux host with the MingGW tools.

Edit the top of the @code{Makefile} if you wish to select specific
options then run @code{make}.

You can type @code{make install} as root if you wish to install the binaries and support files to
@code{/usr/local} (this is not necessary to use QuickJS).

Note: On some OSes atomic operations are not available or need a
specific library. If you get related errors, you should either add
@code{-latomics} in the Makefile @code{LIBS} variable or disable
@code{CONFIG_ATOMICS} in @file{quickjs.c}.

@section Quick start

@code{qjs} is the command line interpreter (Read-Eval-Print Loop). You can pass
Javascript files and/or expressions as arguments to execute them:

@example
./qjs examples/hello.js
@end example

@code{qjsc} is the command line compiler:

@example
./qjsc -o hello examples/hello.js
./hello
@end example

generates a @code{hello} executable with no external dependency.

@section Command line options

@subsection @code{qjs} interpreter

@verbatim
usage: qjs [options] [file [args]]
@end verbatim

Options are:
@table @code
@item -h
@item --help
List options.

@item -v
@item --version
Print the version and exit.

@item -e @code{EXPR}
@item --eval @code{EXPR}
Evaluate EXPR.

@item --typescript
Parse the scripts (including @code{-e}, the standard input given as
file @code{-} and the commands of the interactive mode) as TypeScript.
Files with the @code{.ts}, @code{.mts} and @code{.cts} extensions are
always TypeScript (see the TypeScript section).

@item --experimental-decorators
The TypeScript sources use the legacy decorators (@code{tsc}
@code{experimentalDecorators}) instead of the standard ones. It also
applies to the imported modules, @code{std.evalScript()} and the
workers.

@item --emit-decorator-metadata
Same as @code{--experimental-decorators} and the decorated elements also
get the @code{design:type}, @code{design:paramtypes} and
@code{design:returntype} metadata (@code{tsc}
@code{emitDecoratorMetadata}).

@item -i
@item --interactive
Go to interactive mode (it is not the default when files are provided on the command line).

@item --drepl file
Durable interactive mode. The commands are TypeScript and run in a
separate runtime which is saved to @code{file} with
@code{JS_WriteSnapshot()} after each command, and restored from it
when @code{qjs --drepl file} starts again: the functions, variables
and objects of a session are still there in the next one. The runtime
only has the standard objects, @code{print()} and
@code{console.log()} (not the @code{std} and @code{os} modules, whose
host state cannot be saved). @code{\load} evaluates a file in it.
@code{file} only holds a delta of a new session (see the heap
snapshots): the base, a new session with every builtin instantiated,
is rebuilt at startup.

@item -m
@item --module
Load as ES6 module (default=autodetect). A module is autodetected if
the filename extension is @code{.mjs}, if the first keyword of the
source is @code{import}, or if the source uses top-level @code{await}
and is not a valid script.

@item --script
Load as ES6 script (default=autodetect).

@item -I file
@item --include file
Include an additional file.

@end table

The value of a long option can also be given as
@code{--option=value} (e.g. @code{--memory-limit=16M}).

Advanced options are:

@table @code
@item --std
Make the @code{std}, @code{os} and @code{bjson} modules available to
the loaded script even if it is not a module.

@item -d
@item --dump
Dump the memory usage stats.

@item -D @var{hexmask}
@item --dump-flags @var{hexmask}
Set the debug dump flags (@code{JS_DUMP_xxx} in @file{quickjs.h}, as
an hexadecimal mask, e.g. @code{-D 1} dumps the final bytecode of the
compiled functions). The default is taken from the
@code{QJS_DUMP_FLAGS} environment variable. Ignored unless QuickJS is
compiled with @code{CONFIG_DUMPS=y}.

@item -q
@item --quit
just instantiate the interpreter and quit.

@end table

@subsection @code{qjsc} compiler

@verbatim
usage: qjsc [options] [files]
@end verbatim

Options are:
@table @code
@item -c
Only output bytecode in a C file. The default is to output an executable file.
@item -e
Output @code{main()} and bytecode in a C file. The default is to output an
executable file.
@item -o output
Set the output filename (default = @file{out.c} or @file{a.out}).

@item -N cname
Set the C name of the generated data.

@item -m
Compile as Javascript module (default=autodetect).

@item -D module_name
Compile a dynamically loaded module and its dependencies. This option
is needed when your code uses the @code{import} keyword or the
@code{os.Worker} constructor because the compiler cannot statically
find the name of the dynamically loaded modules.

@item -M module_name[,cname]
Add initialization code for an external C module. See the
@code{c_module} example. The external C modules and the @code{std},
@code{os} and @code{bjson} modules are initialized when they are
statically imported. They are all initialized if the code may import
modules at run time (@code{import()}, @code{eval}, @code{Function},
@code{std.evalScript}, @code{std.loadScript} or @code{os.Worker}) so
that a dynamic import finds them.

@item -x
Byte swapped output (only used for cross compilation).

@item -flto
Use link time optimization. The compilation is slower but the
executable is smaller and faster. This option is automatically set
when the @code{-fno-x} options are used.

@item -fno-[date|eval|string-normalize|regexp|json|proxy|map|typedarray|promise|module-loader|weakref|domexception|atob|performance]
Disable selected language features to produce a smaller executable file.

@item -fno-unused
Disable the features that the compiled code does not reference. A
feature is kept when the code names one of its globals or methods
(e.g. @code{Map}, @code{match} or @code{normalize}), uses a construct
needing it (regexp literals, async functions, modules,
@code{import()}) or imports a @code{std}, @code{os} or @code{bjson}
module whose functions need it (e.g. typed arrays for @code{std}). An
external C module (@code{-M}) keeps every feature, and so does any use
of @code{eval}, @code{Function}, @code{std.evalScript},
@code{std.loadScript}, @code{import()} or @code{os.Worker} because the
code compiled at run time is unknown. Names built at run time (e.g.
@code{globalThis["Ma" +
"p"]}) are not seen: such a feature is missing at run time. With
@code{--experimental}, Temporal and Intl are kept only if
@code{Temporal}, @code{Intl} or a locale method is referenced.

@end table

@section Built-in tests

Run @code{make test} to run the few built-in tests included in the
QuickJS archive.

@section Test262 (ECMAScript Test Suite)

A test262 runner is included in the QuickJS archive. The test262 tests
can be installed in the QuickJS source directory with:

@example
git clone https://github.com/tc39/test262.git test262
cd test262
patch -p1 < ../tests/test262.patch
cd ..
@end example

The patch adds the implementation specific @code{harness} functions
and optimizes the inefficient RegExp character classes and Unicode
property escapes tests (the tests themselves are not modified, only a
slow string initialization function is optimized).

The tests can be run with
@example
make test2
@end example

The configuration files @code{test262.conf}
(resp. @code{test262o.conf} for the old ES5.1 tests@footnote{The old
ES5.1 tests can be extracted with @code{git clone --single-branch
--branch es5-tests https://github.com/tc39/test262.git test262o}}))
contain the options to run the various tests. Tests can be excluded
based on features or filename.

The file @code{test262_errors.txt} contains the current list of
errors. The runner displays a message when a new error appears or when
an existing error is corrected or modified. Use the @code{-u} option
to update the current list of errors (or @code{make test2-update}).

The file @code{test262_report.txt} contains the logs of all the
tests. It is useful to have a clearer analysis of a particular
error. In case of crash, the last line corresponds to the failing
test.

Use the syntax @code{./run-test262 -c test262.conf -f filename.js} to
run a single test. Use the syntax @code{./run-test262 -c test262.conf
N} to start testing at test number @code{N}.

For more information, run @code{./run-test262} to see the command line
options of the test262 runner.

@code{run-test262} accepts the @code{-N} option to be invoked from
@code{test262-harness}@footnote{@url{https://github.com/bterlson/test262-harness}}
thru @code{eshost}. Unless you want to compare QuickJS with other
engines under the same conditions, we do not recommend to run the
tests this way as it is much slower (typically half an hour instead of
about 100 seconds).

@chapter Specifications

@section Language support

@subsection ES2025 support

The ES2025 specification is almost fully supported including the Annex
B (legacy web compatibility) and the Unicode related features.

The following features are not supported yet:

@itemize

@item Tail calls@footnote{We believe the current specification of tails calls is too complicated and presents limited practical interests.}

@item Atomics.waitAsync

@end itemize

The decorators (TC39 proposal, 2023-05 version as implemented by
TypeScript 5) are supported, including the auto-accessors
(@code{accessor} fields), the @code{context} object
(@code{kind}, @code{name}, @code{static}, @code{private},
@code{access}, @code{addInitializer()}, @code{metadata}), the
decorator metadata (@code{Symbol.metadata}) and the class
replacement. They are compiled to bytecode: the methods are defined
before their decorators are applied (as TypeScript and esbuild do) and
the auto-accessors are not enumerable.

The explicit resource management (ES2026) is supported: the
@code{using} and @code{await using} declarations, @code{DisposableStack},
@code{AsyncDisposableStack}, @code{SuppressedError},
@code{Symbol.dispose}, @code{Symbol.asyncDispose},
@code{Iterator.prototype[Symbol.dispose]} and
@code{%AsyncIteratorPrototype%[Symbol.asyncDispose]}. The dispose
methods of the @code{using} declarations are called by the bytecode of
the function, so a suspension (see @code{JS_RequestSuspend()}) can
happen during the disposal, but not during the disposal of a
@code{DisposableStack}.

The immutable ArrayBuffers (TC39 proposal, stage 2.7) are supported:
@code{ArrayBuffer.prototype.transferToImmutable()},
@code{ArrayBuffer.prototype.sliceToImmutable()} and the
@code{ArrayBuffer.prototype.immutable} getter. The contents of an
immutable ArrayBuffer cannot be modified through typed arrays,
@code{DataView} or @code{Atomics}, and the buffer cannot be detached
nor resized. The host can change the immutability of an ArrayBuffer
with @code{JS_SetImmutableArrayBuffer()} (@code{JS_DetachArrayBuffer()}
does nothing on an immutable ArrayBuffer). The binary JSON format does
not keep the immutability: a read ArrayBuffer is always mutable.

@subsection ECMA402

ECMA402 (Internationalization API) is not supported.

The experimental Temporal and Intl implementation (@code{qjs
--experimental}) embeds a subset of the IANA time zone database. The
other time zones are loaded on first use from the system time zone
database: the directory given by the @code{TZDIR} environment variable,
@file{/usr/share/zoneinfo}, @file{/var/db/timezone/zoneinfo} or
@file{/usr/share/lib/zoneinfo}. @code{Intl.supportedValuesOf('timeZone')}
also lists the zones of its @file{tzdata.zi} file. Without a system time
zone database (e.g. on Windows), only the embedded time zones are
available.

@subsection Stack traces

The stack trace of an error is captured when the error object is
created (or, for the errors raised by bytecode operations, when it is
thrown). @code{Error.prototype.stack} is an accessor (Error stack
accessor proposal) returning the captured stack trace: it is not an
own property of the errors. By default it is a string with one
@code{    at function (file:line:column)} line per call level. The
default string is built when the stack is first read, with the call
levels and function names captured at creation time, so that creating
and throwing errors is fast when their stack is not used.

The V8 stack trace API is supported:

@table @code
@item Error.stackTraceLimit
Maximum number of call levels of the stack traces (default = 10). A
value which is not a number disables the stack traces.

@item Error.prepareStackTrace(error, callsites)
If @code{Error.prepareStackTrace} is a function, the stack trace is
its result. @code{callsites} is an array of @code{CallSite} objects
with the methods @code{getFunction()}, @code{getFunctionName()},
@code{getFileName()}, @code{getLineNumber()},
@code{getColumnNumber()} (@code{null} if there is no source position),
@code{isNative()} and @code{isConstructor()}. It is not called for
the errors raised while it runs nor for the errors raised outside of
JS code (e.g. by the host before any JS call): they get the default
stack trace. The default stack trace is also used if it throws. Its
execution cannot be suspended (see @code{JS_RequestSuspend()}).

@item Error.captureStackTrace(obj[, func])
Define @code{obj.stack} as the current stack trace. If @code{func} is
given, the call levels above the last call of @code{func}, and that
call, are omitted.
@end table

@code{Error.stackTraceLimit} and @code{Error.prepareStackTrace} are
read without running JS code: accessor properties are ignored.

@section TypeScript

With the @code{JS_EVAL_FLAG_TYPESCRIPT} flag of @code{JS_Eval()},
QuickJS runs TypeScript sources directly. There is no transpilation
step: the parser erases the types while parsing (no bytecode is
generated for them) and compiles the TypeScript constructs with a
runtime semantic directly to bytecode, following @code{tsc} with the
@code{isolatedModules} and @code{useDefineForClassFields} options. The
generated bytecode is ordinary bytecode. Without the flag, the parser
accepts JavaScript only; direct @code{eval()} code and
@code{Function()} bodies are always JavaScript.

@code{qjs} and the module loader of @code{quickjs-libc} compile the
@code{.ts}, @code{.mts} and @code{.cts} files as TypeScript (a
@code{.mts} file is a module, a @code{.cts} file a script). As in the
usual TypeScript project layout, an import of a missing @code{./x.js}
(or @code{./x}) resolves to an existing @code{./x.ts}, and
@code{./x.mjs} to @code{./x.mts}.

@subsection Type erasure

The TypeScript 5 syntax is supported: annotations of variables,
parameters, return types and class members, optional parameters and
members, definite assignment assertions, @code{this} parameters, type
parameters (with constraints, defaults, @code{const}, @code{in} and
@code{out} modifiers), type arguments of calls, @code{new}, tagged
templates and instantiation expressions, @code{as}, @code{as const},
@code{satisfies}, non-null assertions, @code{<T>x} assertions, type
aliases, interfaces, @code{declare} declarations, overload
signatures, abstract classes and members, accessibility,
@code{readonly}, @code{override} and @code{declare} modifiers, index
signatures, @code{implements}, @code{import type}, @code{export type}
and the inline @code{type} specifiers, and the complete type grammar.
The contextual keywords (@code{type}, @code{namespace}, @code{declare},
@code{abstract}, @code{as}, ...) remain identifiers where JavaScript
allows them, with the line break rules of @code{tsc}. The ambiguities
(@code{f<T>(x)} versus comparisons, arrow functions with a return
type, including in the middle operand of @code{?:}) are resolved with
the rules of @code{tsc} (as implemented by esbuild).

@subsection Runtime constructs

@table @code
@item enum
Compiled like @code{tsc}: @code{var E; (function (E) @{ ... @})(E ||
(E = @{@}))}, with reverse mappings for the non string members,
auto-incremented values, references to the other members in the
initializers and merging of the declarations. The constant member
initializers are evaluated at compile time.

@item const enum
The members must be constant expressions. Each @code{E.A},
@code{E["A"]} or @code{N.E.A} of the same compilation unit that
statically resolves to the enum is compiled to the constant (this is
done when the variables are resolved, so the uses before the
declaration and the shadowing are handled). The enum object is also
emitted (as with @code{preserveConstEnums}) so that dynamic accesses
and exports work.

@item namespace, module
Compiled like @code{tsc}, including dotted names, merging of the
blocks and with functions, classes and enums, and @code{import X =
N.Y} aliases. An exported variable is a property of the namespace
object: its references in all the blocks of the namespace are compiled
as property accesses. Namespaces without values emit nothing.

@item Parameter properties
@code{constructor(public x)} defines the field @code{x} before the
other fields and assigns it at the start of the constructor, after
@code{super()} in a derived class. Class fields have the standard
semantics: a field without initializer (@code{x: number;}) is
initialized to @code{undefined}, @code{declare x: number;} emits
nothing.

@item Import elision
In a module, an imported binding that is never used as a value is
dropped, and an import declaration whose bindings were all dropped is
not loaded (except @code{import "x"}). @code{export @{ T @}} of a name
that is only a type (interface, type alias, type-only import) is
dropped. A name both imported and declared locally refers to the local
declaration.
@end table

@subsection Decorators

By default the decorators are the standard ones (see the ES2025
support section). With @code{JS_EVAL_FLAG_TS_LEGACY_DECORATORS}
(@code{qjs --experimental-decorators}), they follow @code{tsc}
@code{experimentalDecorators}: the decorator expressions are evaluated
after the class definition, then the decorators of the instance
members, of the static members and of the class are applied in this
order as with the @code{__decorate()} helper (including
@code{Reflect.decorate()} when it exists). Parameter decorators are
supported for methods and constructors. The returned descriptors are
defined and the returned class replaces the class (the class name
refers to it inside the class). With
@code{JS_EVAL_FLAG_TS_DECORATOR_METADATA} (@code{qjs
--emit-decorator-metadata}), the @code{design:type},
@code{design:paramtypes} and @code{design:returntype} metadata are
computed from the type annotations as @code{tsc} does with
@code{strictNullChecks} (a type reference gives its value if it is a
function at run time, @code{Object} otherwise; the enums declared
before the class give @code{Number} or @code{String}) and defined with
@code{Reflect.metadata()} when it exists at run time (e.g. with the
@code{reflect-metadata} package).

@subsection Limitations

@itemize
@item There is no type checking.
@item JSX/TSX, @code{export =} and @code{import x =
require()} (CommonJS) are rejected with a SyntaxError.
@item Decorators of @code{declare} and @code{abstract} members and of
overload signatures are ignored. Legacy decorator expressions cannot
contain @code{await} or @code{yield}.
@item As with @code{isolatedModules}, the type of the imported names
is unknown: re-exporting an imported type requires @code{export type},
and the const enums of other modules are accessed as objects.
@item The code relying on @code{useDefineForClassFields: false} (typed
fields without initializer that do not shadow inherited properties)
behaves differently.
@item The members of @code{declare const enum} are not folded.
@item An @code{import X = A.B} alias is evaluated at the declaration
but does not throw if @code{A} is not a value (type-only namespace).
@item The non constant enum members get a reverse mapping even when
their value is a string.
@end itemize

@section Modules

ES6 modules are fully supported. The default name resolution is the
following:

@itemize

@item Module names with a leading @code{.} or @code{..} are relative
to the current module path.

@item Module names without a leading @code{.} or @code{..} are system
modules, such as @code{std} or @code{os}.

@item Module names ending with @code{.so} are native modules using the
QuickJS C API.

@end itemize

The @code{type} import attribute selects how the module file is
loaded by @code{qjs}:

@itemize

@item @code{"json"} (default for module names ending with
@code{.json}): the default export is the parsed JSON value.

@item @code{"json5"}: same as @code{"json"} with the extensions of
@code{std.parseExtJSON()}.

@item @code{"text"}: the default export is the file contents as a
string (UTF-8 encoding).

@item @code{"bytes"}: the default export is the file contents as an
@code{Uint8Array} backed by an immutable ArrayBuffer.

@end itemize

Example:
@example
import text from "./README.md" with @{ type: "text" @};
@end example

Other types are rejected with a @code{TypeError}.

@section Standard library

The standard library is included by default in the command line
interpreter. It contains the three modules @code{std}, @code{os} and
@code{bjson} and a few global objects.

@subsection Global objects

@table @code
@item scriptArgs
Provides the command line arguments. The first argument is the script name.
@item print(...args)
Print the arguments separated by spaces and a trailing newline.
@item console.log(...args)
Same as print().

@end table

The @code{qjs} interpreter (and its workers) also defines:

@table @code
@item gc()
Same as @code{std.gc()}.
@item execArgv
The whole command line of @code{qjs}, options included, as an array
of strings.
@item argv0
The first element of @code{execArgv} (the name used to run @code{qjs}).
@item navigator.userAgent
The string @code{"quickjs/"} followed by the QuickJS version.
@end table

@code{performance.now()} and @code{performance.timeOrigin} are part of
the engine (@code{JS_AddPerformance()}, added by @code{JS_NewContext()}).

@subsection @code{std} module

The @code{std} module provides wrappers to the libc @file{stdlib.h}
and @file{stdio.h} and a few other utilities.

Available exports:

@table @code

@item exit(n)
Exit the process.

@item evalScript(str, options = undefined)
Evaluate the string @code{str} as a script (global
eval). @code{options} is an optional object containing the following
optional properties:

  @table @code
  @item backtrace_barrier
  Boolean (default = false). If true, error backtraces do not list the
  stack frames below the evalScript.
  @item compile_only
  Boolean (default = false). If true, @code{str} is only compiled and
  the compiled script (or module with @code{compile_module}) is
  returned without being run. It can be serialized with
  @code{bjson.write(obj, bjson.WRITE_OBJ_BYTECODE)}.
  @item compile_module
  Boolean (default = false). If true, @code{str} is evaluated (or
  compiled with @code{compile_only}) as a module.
  @item eval_function
  Boolean (default = false). If true, @code{str} is a script returned
  by @code{compile_only} (or read back by @code{bjson.read()}) and it
  is run.
  @item eval_module
  Boolean (default = false). If true, @code{str} is a module returned
  by @code{compile_only} and @code{compile_module}: its imports are
  resolved, then it is evaluated and the promise of its evaluation is
  returned.
  @item async
  Boolean (default = false). If true, @code{await} is accepted in the
  script and a promise is returned. The promise is resolved with an
  object whose @code{value} property holds the value returned by the
  script.
  @item typescript
  Boolean (default = false). If true, the script is TypeScript.
  @item experimentalDecorators
  @item emitDecoratorMetadata
  Booleans (default = the @code{--experimental-decorators} and
  @code{--emit-decorator-metadata} settings, see
  @code{js_std_set_typescript_flags()}). TypeScript legacy decorators
  and their metadata.
  @end table

@item loadScript(filename)
Evaluate the file @code{filename} as a script (global eval).

@item loadFile(filename)
Load the file @code{filename} and return it as a string assuming UTF-8
encoding. Return @code{null} in case of I/O error.

@item writeFile(filename, data)
Create or truncate the file @code{filename} and write @code{data} to
it: the bytes of an ArrayBuffer or of a typed array (only the bytes of
its view), otherwise @code{data} converted to a string in UTF-8
(nothing if @code{data} is undefined). Throw an exception in case of
I/O error.

@item open(filename, flags, errorObj = undefined)
Open a file (wrapper to the libc @code{fopen()}). @code{flags} is
made of the characters @code{rwa+bx} (@code{x}: fail if the file
exists). Return the FILE
object or @code{null} in case of I/O error. If @code{errorObj} is not
undefined, set its @code{errno} property to the error code or to 0 if
no error occured.

@item popen(command, flags, errorObj = undefined)
Open a process by creating a pipe (wrapper to the libc
@code{popen()}). @code{flags} is @code{"r"} or @code{"w"}. Return the FILE
object or @code{null} in case of I/O error. If @code{errorObj} is not
undefined, set its @code{errno} property to the error code or to 0 if
no error occured.

@item fdopen(fd, flags, errorObj = undefined)
Open a file from a file handle (wrapper to the libc
@code{fdopen()}). Return the FILE
object or @code{null} in case of I/O error. If @code{errorObj} is not
undefined, set its @code{errno} property to the error code or to 0 if
no error occured.

@item tmpfile(errorObj = undefined)
Open a temporary file. Return the FILE
object or @code{null} in case of I/O error. If @code{errorObj} is not
undefined, set its @code{errno} property to the error code or to 0 if
no error occured.

@item puts(str)
Equivalent to @code{std.out.puts(str)}.

@item printf(fmt, ...args)
Equivalent to @code{std.out.printf(fmt, ...args)}.

@item sprintf(fmt, ...args)
Equivalent to the libc sprintf().

@item in
@item out
@item err
Wrappers to the libc file @code{stdin}, @code{stdout}, @code{stderr}.
They cannot be closed: @code{close()} throws a @code{TypeError}.

@item SEEK_SET
@item SEEK_CUR
@item SEEK_END
Constants for seek().

@item Error

Enumeration object containing the integer value of common errors
(additional error codes may be defined):

  @table @code
  @item EINVAL
  @item EIO
  @item EACCES
  @item EEXIST
  @item ENOSPC
  @item ENOSYS
  @item EBUSY
  @item ENOENT
  @item EPERM
  @item EPIPE
  @end table

@item strerror(errno)
Return a string that describes the error @code{errno}.

@item gc()
Manually invoke the cycle removal algorithm. The cycle removal
algorithm is automatically started when needed, so this function is
useful in case of specific memory constraints or for testing.

@item getenv(name)
Return the value of the environment variable @code{name} or
@code{undefined} if it is not defined.

@item setenv(name, value)
Set the value of the environment variable @code{name} to the string
@code{value}.

@item unsetenv(name)
Delete the environment variable @code{name}.

@item getenviron()
Return an object containing the environment variables as key-value pairs.

@item urlGet(url, options = undefined)

Download @code{url} using the @file{curl} command line
utility. @code{options} is an optional object containing the following
optional properties:

  @table @code
  @item binary
  Boolean (default = false). If true, the response is an ArrayBuffer
  instead of a string. When a string is returned, the data is assumed
  to be UTF-8 encoded.

  @item full

  Boolean (default = false). If true, return the an object contains
  the properties @code{response} (response content),
  @code{responseHeaders} (headers separated by CRLF), @code{status}
  (status code). @code{response} is @code{null} is case of protocol or
  network error. If @code{full} is false, only the response is
  returned if the status is between 200 and 299. Otherwise @code{null}
  is returned.

  @end table

@item parseExtJSON(str)

  Parse @code{str} using a superset of @code{JSON.parse}. The superset
  is very close to the JSON5 specification. The following extensions
  are accepted:

  @itemize
  @item Single line and multiline comments
  @item unquoted properties (ASCII-only Javascript identifiers)
  @item trailing comma in array and object definitions
  @item single quoted strings
  @item @code{\v} escape and multi-line strings with trailing @code{\}
  @item @code{\f} and @code{\v} are accepted as space characters
  @item leading plus or decimal point in numbers
  @item hexadecimal (@code{0x} prefix), octal (@code{0o} prefix) and binary (@code{0b} prefix) integers
  @item @code{NaN} and @code{Infinity} are accepted as numbers
  @end itemize
@end table

FILE prototype:

@table @code
@item close()
Close the file. Return 0 if OK or @code{-errno} in case of I/O error.
@item puts(str)
Outputs the string with the UTF-8 encoding.
@item printf(fmt, ...args)
Formatted printf.

The same formats as the standard C library @code{printf} are
supported. Integer format types (e.g. @code{%d}) truncate the Numbers
or BigInts to 32 bits. Use the @code{l} modifier (e.g. @code{%ld}) to
truncate to 64 bits.

@item flush()
Flush the buffered file.
@item seek(offset, whence)
Seek to a give file position (whence is
@code{std.SEEK_*}). @code{offset} can be a number or a bigint. Return
0 if OK or @code{-errno} in case of I/O error.
@item tell()
Return the current file position.
@item tello()
Return the current file position as a bigint.
@item eof()
Return true if end of file.
@item fileno()
Return the associated OS handle.
@item error()
Return true if there was an error.
@item clearerr()
Clear the error indication.

@item read(buffer, position = 0, length = buffer.byteLength - position)
Read @code{length} bytes from the file to the ArrayBuffer @code{buffer} at byte
position @code{position} (wrapper to the libc @code{fread}). The
range is clamped to the buffer. @code{buffer} must not be immutable.
Return the number of bytes read.

@item write(buffer, position = 0, length = buffer.byteLength - position)
Write @code{length} bytes to the file from the ArrayBuffer @code{buffer} at byte
position @code{position} (wrapper to the libc @code{fwrite}). If
@code{buffer} is a string, its UTF-8 bytes are written and
@code{position} and @code{length} count bytes. The range is clamped to
the buffer. Return the number of bytes written.

@item getline()
Return the next line from the file, assuming UTF-8 encoding, excluding
the trailing line feed.

@item readAsString(max_size = undefined)
Read @code{max_size} bytes from the file and return them as a string
assuming UTF-8 encoding. If @code{max_size} is not present, the file
is read up its end.

@item readAsArrayBuffer(max_size = undefined)
Same as @code{readAsString()} but return the bytes as an ArrayBuffer.

@item getByte()
Return the next byte from the file. Return -1 if the end of file is reached.

@item putByte(c)
Write one byte to the file.
@end table

@subsection @code{os} module

The @code{os} module provides Operating System specific functions:

@itemize
@item low level file access
@item signals
@item timers
@item asynchronous I/O
@item workers (threads)
@end itemize

The OS functions usually return 0 if OK or an OS specific negative
error code.

Available exports:

@table @code
@item open(filename, flags, mode = 0o666)
Open a file. Return a handle or < 0 if error.

@item O_RDONLY
@item O_WRONLY
@item O_RDWR
@item O_APPEND
@item O_CREAT
@item O_EXCL
@item O_TRUNC
POSIX open flags.

@item O_TEXT
(Windows specific). Open the file in text mode. The default is binary mode.

@item close(fd)
Close the file handle @code{fd}.

@item seek(fd, offset, whence)
Seek in the file. Use @code{std.SEEK_*} for
@code{whence}. @code{offset} is either a number or a bigint. If
@code{offset} is a bigint, a bigint is returned too.

@item read(fd, buffer, offset, length)
Read @code{length} bytes from the file handle @code{fd} to the
ArrayBuffer @code{buffer} at byte position @code{offset}. @code{buffer}
must not be immutable.
Return the number of read bytes or < 0 if error.

@item write(fd, buffer, offset, length)
Write @code{length} bytes to the file handle @code{fd} from the
ArrayBuffer @code{buffer} at byte position @code{offset}.
Return the number of written bytes or < 0 if error.

@item isatty(fd)
Return @code{true} is @code{fd} is a TTY (terminal) handle.

@item ttyGetWinSize(fd)
Return the TTY size as @code{[width, height]} or @code{null} if not available.

@item ttySetRaw(fd)
Set the TTY in raw mode.

@item remove(filename)
Remove a file. Return 0 if OK or @code{-errno}.

@item rename(oldname, newname)
Rename a file. Return 0 if OK or @code{-errno}.

@item realpath(path)
Return @code{[str, err]} where @code{str} is the canonicalized absolute
pathname of @code{path} and @code{err} the error code.

@item exePath()
Return the absolute path of the running executable or
@code{undefined} if it is not available.

@item getcwd()
Return @code{[str, err]} where @code{str} is the current working directory
and @code{err} the error code.

@item chdir(path)
Change the current directory. Return 0 if OK or @code{-errno}.

@item mkdir(path, mode = 0o777)
Create a directory at @code{path}. Return 0 if OK or @code{-errno}.

@item mkdtemp(pattern = "tmpXXXXXX")
Create a directory with a unique name and the mode @code{0o700}. The
trailing @code{XXXXXX} of @code{pattern} (appended if missing) is
replaced to make the name unique. Return @code{[path, err]} where
@code{path} is the directory name and @code{err} the error code. Not
available on Windows.

@item mkstemp(pattern = "tmpXXXXXX")
Create and open for reading and writing a file with a unique name and
the mode @code{0o600}, as @code{mkdtemp()}. Return @code{[path, fd]}
where @code{fd} is the file handle or @code{-errno} in case of
error. The file is not removed when it is closed. Not available on
Windows.

@item stat(path)
@item lstat(path)

Return @code{[obj, err]} where @code{obj} is an object containing the
file status of @code{path}. @code{err} is the error code. The
following fields are defined in @code{obj}: dev, ino, mode, nlink,
uid, gid, rdev, size, blocks, atime, mtime, ctime. The times are
specified in milliseconds since 1970. @code{lstat()} is the same as
@code{stat()} excepts that it returns information about the link
itself.

@item S_IFMT
@item S_IFIFO
@item S_IFCHR
@item S_IFDIR
@item S_IFBLK
@item S_IFREG
@item S_IFSOCK
@item S_IFLNK
@item S_ISGID
@item S_ISUID
Constants to interpret the @code{mode} property returned by
@code{stat()}. They have the same value as in the C system header
@file{sys/stat.h}.

@item utimes(path, atime, mtime)
Change the access and modification times of the file @code{path}. The
times are specified in milliseconds since 1970. Return 0 if OK or @code{-errno}.

@item symlink(target, linkpath)
Create a link at @code{linkpath} containing the string @code{target}. Return 0 if OK or @code{-errno}.

@item readlink(path)
Return @code{[str, err]} where @code{str} is the link target and @code{err}
the error code.

@item readdir(path)
Return @code{[array, err]} where @code{array} is an array of strings
containing the filenames of the directory @code{path}. @code{err} is
the error code.

@item setReadHandler(fd, func)
Add a read handler to the file handle @code{fd}. @code{func} is called
each time there is data pending for @code{fd}. A single read handler
per file handle is supported. Use @code{func = null} to remove the
handler.

@item setWriteHandler(fd, func)
Add a write handler to the file handle @code{fd}. @code{func} is
called each time data can be written to @code{fd}. A single write
handler per file handle is supported. Use @code{func = null} to remove
the handler.

@item signal(signal, func)
Call the function @code{func} when the signal @code{signal}
happens. Only a single handler per signal number is supported. Use
@code{null} to set the default handler or @code{undefined} to ignore
the signal. Signal handlers can only be defined in the main thread.

@item SIGINT
@item SIGABRT
@item SIGFPE
@item SIGILL
@item SIGSEGV
@item SIGTERM
POSIX signal numbers.

@item kill(pid, sig)
Send the signal @code{sig} to the process @code{pid}.

@item exec(args[, options])
Execute a process with the arguments @code{args}. @code{options} is an
object containing optional parameters:

  @table @code
  @item block
  Boolean (default = true). If true, wait until the process is
  terminated. In this case, @code{exec} return the exit code if positive
  or the negated signal number if the process was interrupted by a
  signal. If false, do not block and return the process id of the child.

  @item usePath
  Boolean (default = true). If true, the file is searched in the
  @code{PATH} environment variable.

  @item file
  String (default = @code{args[0]}). Set the file to be executed.

  @item cwd
  String. If present, set the working directory of the new process.

  @item stdin
  @item stdout
  @item stderr
  If present, set the handle in the child for stdin, stdout or stderr.

  @item env
  Object. If present, set the process environment from the object
  key-value pairs. Otherwise use the same environment as the current
  process.

  @item uid
  Integer. If present, the process uid with @code{setuid}.

  @item gid
  Integer. If present, the process gid with @code{setgid}.

  @end table

@item getpid()
Return the current process ID.

@item waitpid(pid, options)
@code{waitpid} Unix system call. Return the array @code{[ret,
status]}. @code{ret} contains @code{-errno} in case of error.

@item WNOHANG
Constant for the @code{options} argument of @code{waitpid}.

@item dup(fd)
@code{dup} Unix system call.

@item dup2(oldfd, newfd)
@code{dup2} Unix system call.

@item pipe()
@code{pipe} Unix system call. Return two handles as @code{[read_fd,
write_fd]} or null in case of error.

@item sleep(delay_ms)
Sleep during @code{delay_ms} milliseconds.

@item sleepAsync(delay_ms)
Asynchronouse sleep during @code{delay_ms} milliseconds. Returns a promise. Example:
@example
await os.sleepAsync(500);
@end example

@item now()
Return a timestamp in milliseconds with more precision than
@code{Date.now()}. The time origin is unspecified and is normally not
impacted by system clock adjustments.

@item cputime()
Return the user CPU time of the process in microseconds (not
available on Windows).

@item setTimeout(func, delay)
Call the function @code{func} after @code{delay} ms. Return a handle
to the timer.

@item clearTimeout(handle)
Cancel a timer.

@item setInterval(func, delay)
Call the function @code{func} every @code{delay} ms (at least 1 ms)
until the timer is cancelled. Return a handle to the timer.

@item clearInterval(handle)
Cancel a timer. @code{clearTimeout()} and @code{clearInterval()} accept
the handles of both kinds of timers.

@item platform
Return a string representing the platform: @code{"linux"}, @code{"darwin"},
@code{"win32"} or @code{"js"}.

@item Worker(module_filename)
Constructor to create a new thread (worker) with an API close to the
@code{WebWorkers}. @code{module_filename} is a string specifying the
module filename which is executed in the newly created thread. As for
dynamically imported module, it is relative to the current script or
module path. Threads normally don't share any data and communicate
between each other with messages. Nested workers are not supported. An
example is available in @file{tests/test_worker.js}.

The worker class has the following static properties:

  @table @code
  @item parent
  In the created worker, @code{Worker.parent} represents the parent
  worker and is used to send or receive messages.
  @end table

The worker instances have the following properties:

  @table @code
  @item postMessage(msg)

  Send a message to the corresponding worker. @code{msg} is cloned in
  the destination worker using an algorithm similar to the @code{HTML}
  structured clone algorithm. @code{SharedArrayBuffer} are shared
  between workers. The byte length of a growable
  @code{SharedArrayBuffer} is also shared: @code{grow()} in a worker
  is visible from the others.

  Current limitations: @code{Map} and @code{Set} are not supported
  yet.

  @item onmessage

  Getter and setter. Set a function which is called each time a
  message is received. The function is called with a single
  argument. It is an object with a @code{data} property containing the
  received message. The thread is not terminated if there is at least
  one non @code{null} @code{onmessage} handler.

  @end table

@end table

@subsection @code{bjson} module

The @code{bjson} module reads and writes the binary object format of
@code{JS_ReadObject()} and @code{JS_WriteObject()} (see Binary
JSON).

Available exports:

@table @code
@item write(obj, flags = 0)
Serialize @code{obj} and return an ArrayBuffer. @code{flags} is a
combination of @code{WRITE_OBJ_BYTECODE} (allow functions and
modules), @code{WRITE_OBJ_REFERENCE} (allow shared and circular
object references), @code{WRITE_OBJ_STRIP_SOURCE} (omit the source of
the functions) and @code{WRITE_OBJ_STRIP_DEBUG} (also omit the file
names and line number tables).

@item read(buffer, position, length, flags = 0)
Deserialize the @code{length} bytes at byte position @code{position}
of the ArrayBuffer @code{buffer}. @code{flags} is a combination of
@code{READ_OBJ_BYTECODE} and @code{READ_OBJ_REFERENCE}, which must
match the flags given to @code{write()}. Bytecode is trusted input:
only read the bytecode you wrote. A SyntaxError is thrown if
@code{buffer} was written by another version of the format
(@code{invalid version}) or if it is corrupted (@code{checksum
error}).

@item READ_OBJ_BYTECODE
@item READ_OBJ_REFERENCE
@item WRITE_OBJ_BYTECODE
@item WRITE_OBJ_REFERENCE
@item WRITE_OBJ_STRIP_SOURCE
@item WRITE_OBJ_STRIP_DEBUG
Constants for the @code{flags} arguments.
@end table

@section QuickJS C API

The C API was designed to be simple and efficient. The C API is
defined in the header @code{quickjs.h}.

@subsection Runtime and contexts

@code{JSRuntime} represents a Javascript runtime corresponding to an
object heap. Several runtimes can exist at the same time but they
cannot exchange objects. Inside a given runtime, no multi-threading is
supported.

@code{JSContext} represents a Javascript context (or Realm). Each
JSContext has its own global objects and system objects. There can be
several JSContexts per JSRuntime and they can share objects, similar
to frames of the same origin sharing Javascript objects in a
web browser.

@subsection JSValue

@code{JSValue} represents a Javascript value which can be a primitive
type or an object. Reference counting is used, so it is important to
explicitly duplicate (@code{JS_DupValue()}, increment the reference
count) or free (@code{JS_FreeValue()}, decrement the reference count)
JSValues.

@code{JS_FreeValues()} and @code{JS_FreeAtoms()} free up to 8 values
or atoms in a single call.

Strings can be exchanged as UTF-16 code units with
@code{JS_NewStringUTF16()} and @code{JS_ToCStringLenUTF16()} (released
with @code{JS_FreeCStringUTF16()}). Unlike the UTF-8 functions, they
preserve unpaired surrogates. @code{JS_NewStringLatin1Len()} creates a
string from Latin-1 bytes. @code{JS_GetStringData()} returns the
contents of a string without copying or encoding them: Latin-1 bytes or
UTF-16 code units depending on the internal representation. A rope is
flattened in place and the pointer is valid while the string value is
held.

@code{JS_NewUint8Array()} and @code{JS_NewUint8ArrayCopy()} create a
@code{Uint8Array} over a new @code{ArrayBuffer};
@code{JS_GetUint8Array()} returns the bytes viewed by a
@code{Uint8Array}, taking its offset into account.
@code{JS_GetTypedArrayData()} returns the bytes viewed by a typed array
of any element type with their byte length, byte offset and element
size. It does not reference the @code{ArrayBuffer} and does not raise
an exception: it returns @code{NULL} if the value is not a typed array
(see @code{JS_GetTypedArrayType()}), or if the view is out of bounds or
its buffer is detached.

@code{JS_GetPropertyValue()} and @code{JS_SetPropertyValue()} access a
property whose key is an arbitrary @code{JSValue} (freed by the
call). @code{JS_SetLength()} assigns the @code{length} property, and
@code{JS_SealObject()}/@code{JS_FreezeObject()} behave like
@code{Object.seal()}/@code{Object.freeze()}.

@code{JS_FindAtomLen()} returns the atom of a UTF-8 string only if it
already exists (@code{JS_ATOM_NULL} otherwise) and never allocates
memory. @code{JS_GetPropertyStrLen()} and @code{JS_HasPropertyStrLen()}
take a UTF-8 property name with an explicit length; like
@code{JS_GetPropertyStr()}, they do not create a temporary atom when
the name is not an atom yet and no exotic object (such as a
@code{Proxy}) in the prototype chain can observe it.

@code{JS_GetOwnPropertyCount()} returns the number of properties
@code{JS_GetOwnPropertyNames()} would return with the same flags,
without building the list for ordinary objects.

@code{JS_NewArrayFrom()} creates an array from a C array of values
(taking ownership of them). @code{JS_GetFastArray()} gives direct read
access to the elements of an array without holes: the returned pointer
is borrowed and only valid until the array is modified or any
Javascript code (getter, setter, Proxy trap, finalizer...) may run, so
the elements should be duplicated with @code{JS_DupValue()} before
doing anything else. Proxies, typed arrays and arrays with holes are
not handled: use @code{JS_GetPropertyUint32()} for them.

@subsection C functions

C functions can be created with
@code{JS_NewCFunction()}. @code{JS_SetPropertyFunctionList()} is a
shortcut to easily add functions, setters and getters properties to a
given object. @code{JS_CFUNC_DEF2()}, @code{JS_CGETSET_DEF2()} and
@code{JS_CGETSET_MAGIC_DEF2()} are variants of the list entry macros
taking explicit property flags. @code{JS_NewCFunction3()} also takes the prototype of the
function, @code{JS_NewCFunctionData2()} creates a named function with
JavaScript values bound to it. @code{JS_NewCClosure()} binds a C
pointer instead: the closure gets the @code{opaque} pointer on each
call and its finalizer is called with it when the function is freed.
@code{JS_WriteSnapshot()} fails with a @code{TypeError} if a C closure
is reachable (its pointer is host state).

Unlike other embedded Javascript engines, there is no implicit stack,
so C functions get their parameters as normal C parameters. As a
general rule, C functions take constant @code{JSValue}s as parameters
(so they don't need to free them) and return a newly allocated (=live)
@code{JSValue}.

@code{JS_NewPrivateSymbol()} creates a symbol for host data attached to
objects: like the private class members, its properties are never
listed (@code{Reflect.ownKeys()}, @code{Object.getOwnPropertySymbols()},
@code{JS_WriteObject()}...) and they are always own properties of the
object, also on a Proxy (no trap is called) or on an object with exotic
methods. Only C code holding the symbol can read them.

@subsection Exceptions

Exceptions: most C functions can return a Javascript exception. It
must be explicitly tested and handled by the C code. The specific
@code{JSValue} @code{JS_EXCEPTION} indicates that an exception
occurred. The actual exception object is stored in the
@code{JSContext} and can be retrieved with @code{JS_GetException()}.

@code{JS_NewTypeError()}, @code{JS_NewRangeError()} and the other
@code{JS_New*Error()} functions build the same error objects as their
@code{JS_Throw*Error()} counterparts but return them without throwing.
Their message is formatted with @code{printf()} semantics and is not
truncated.

@code{JS_NewErrorLen()} and @code{JS_ThrowErrorLen()} take the kind
of error (@code{JSErrorEnum}, e.g. @code{JS_TYPE_ERROR} or
@code{JS_PLAIN_ERROR} for @code{Error}) and a UTF-8 message with its
length: no formatting is done and the message may contain any
character.

@subsection Script evaluation

Use @code{JS_Eval()} to evaluate a script or module source.
@code{JS_Eval2()} and @code{JS_EvalThis2()} take a
@code{JSEvalOptions} structure (@code{version} must be
@code{JS_EVAL_OPTIONS_VERSION}) with the evaluation flags, including
@code{JS_EVAL_FLAG_TYPESCRIPT} and the decorator flags, the file name
and the line number of the first line of the source.

If the script or module was compiled to bytecode with @code{qjsc}, it
can be evaluated by calling @code{js_std_eval_binary()}. The advantage
is that no compilation is needed so it is faster and smaller because
the compiler can be removed from the executable if no @code{eval} is
required.

Note: the bytecode format is linked to a given QuickJS
version. Moreover, no security check is done before its
execution. Hence the bytecode should not be loaded from untrusted
sources. That's why there is no option to output the bytecode to a
binary file in @code{qjsc}.

@subsection JS Classes

C opaque data can be attached to a Javascript object. The type of the
C opaque data is determined with the class ID (@code{JSClassID}) of
the object. Hence the first step is to register a new class ID and JS
class (@code{JS_NewClassID()}, @code{JS_NewClass()}). Then you can
create objects of this class with @code{JS_NewObjectClass()} and get or
set the C opaque point with
@code{JS_GetOpaque()}/@code{JS_SetOpaque()}.

When defining a new JS class, it is possible to declare a finalizer
which is called when the object is destroyed. The finalizer should be
used to release C resources. It is invalid to execute JS code from
it. A @code{gc_mark} method can be provided so that the cycle removal
algorithm can find the other objects referenced by this object. Other
methods are available to define exotic object behaviors.

The Class ID are globally allocated (i.e. for all runtimes). The
JSClass are allocated per @code{JSRuntime}. @code{JS_SetClassProto()}
is used to define a prototype for a given class in a given
JSContext. @code{JS_NewObjectClass()} sets this prototype in the
created object. @code{JS_GetClassName()} returns the name of a
registered class as an atom.

Examples are available in @file{quickjs-libc.c}.

@subsection C Modules

Native ES6 modules are supported and can be dynamically or statically
linked. Look at the @file{examples/point.c} and
@file{examples/fib.c} examples. The standard library
@file{quickjs-libc.c} is also a good example of a native module.

@subsection Standard library C API

@file{quickjs-libc.h} gives the C API of the standard library:

@table @code
@item js_init_module_std(ctx, name)
@item js_init_module_os(ctx, name)
@item js_init_module_bjson(ctx, name)
Declare the @code{std}, @code{os} and @code{bjson} modules under the
module name @code{name}.

@item js_std_init_handlers(rt)
@item js_std_free_handlers(rt)
Allocate and free the state of the @code{os} timers, handlers and
workers of a runtime.

@item js_module_loader(ctx, name, opaque, attributes)
@item js_module_load(ctx, name, opaque, attributes, load_file)
Module loaders: @code{js_module_load()} is @code{js_module_loader()}
without the shared library (@code{.so}) modules and with the file
reader @code{load_file} (@code{js_load_file()} for
@code{js_module_loader()}).

@item js_std_loop(ctx)
Run the jobs, timers and handlers until there is nothing left to do.
The exceptions of the handlers are printed.

@item js_std_loop_once(ctx)
@item js_std_poll_io(ctx, timeout_ms)
The steps of @code{js_std_loop()} for a host which runs its own event
loop. @code{js_std_loop_once()} runs the pending jobs, then the handler
of at most one expired timer, and returns 0 if there is more to do
now, the delay in ms until the next timer, or -1 if there is no job
and no timer. @code{js_std_poll_io()} waits at most @code{timeout_ms}
ms (forever if negative, unless there is nothing to wait for) for a
signal, a file descriptor of @code{os.setReadHandler()} or
@code{os.setWriteHandler()}, or a worker message, and runs its
handler. It returns 0, or -1 if @code{poll()} failed. Both return -2
if a job or handler threw: the exception is left pending
(@code{JS_GetException()}). A host loop:
@example
for(;;) @{
    ret = js_std_loop_once(ctx);
    if (ret == -2)
        handle_exception(ctx);
    else if (ret != 0)
        js_std_poll_io(ctx, ret); /* ret == -1: no timer */
@}
@end example

@item js_std_set_worker_new_runtime_func(func)
@item js_std_set_worker_new_context_func(func)
Set the functions creating the runtime (@code{JS_NewRuntime()} by
default) and the context of the new workers. Without context function,
the @code{os.Worker} constructor throws a @code{TypeError}.
@end table

The module name normalizer set with @code{JS_SetModuleLoaderFunc()} or
@code{JS_SetModuleLoaderFunc2()} can be replaced, after them, by
@code{JS_SetModuleNormalizeFunc2()}, whose normalizer also gets the
import attributes (an object, or @code{undefined} without
@code{with} clause). Modules are keyed by their normalized name and
their attributes.

@subsection Memory handling

Use @code{JS_SetMemoryLimit()} to set a global memory allocation limit
to a given JSRuntime.

Custom memory allocation functions can be provided with
@code{JS_NewRuntime2()}. The @code{js_calloc} member of
@code{JSMallocFunctions} is required: the engine uses it for its zero
initialized allocations (@code{js_calloc()}, @code{js_mallocz()}...).

@code{JS_GetGCThreshold()} returns the allocation size triggering the
next automatic garbage collection. Functions registered with
@code{JS_AddRuntimeFinalizer()} are called in reverse order at the end
of @code{JS_FreeRuntime()}, for example to release host data shared by
the finalizers of the objects.

The maximum system stack size can be set with @code{JS_SetMaxStackSize()}.

Debug dumps (bytecode, GC, leaks, module resolution, ...) on stdout
are selected per runtime with @code{JS_SetDumpFlags()} and the
@code{JS_DUMP_xxx} flags (@code{JS_GetDumpFlags()} returns them). They
are only compiled if @code{ENABLE_DUMPS} is defined (Makefile:
@code{CONFIG_DUMPS=y}); otherwise the flags are ignored and
@code{JS_GetDumpFlags()} returns 0. @code{JS_ABORT_ON_LEAKS} makes
@code{JS_FreeRuntime()} abort on leaked objects, strings or atoms.

@subsection Pending jobs

Promise reactions and other jobs are queued in the runtime and run by
the host. @code{JS_ExecutePendingJob(rt, &ctx)} runs the oldest one and
returns 1, 0 if there was none, or -1 if it threw: @code{ctx} is then
the context of the job, where the exception is pending (NULL if the
job destroyed it). @code{JS_ExecutePendingJobs(rt, max_jobs, &ctx)}
does the same in a loop, in one call: it runs the jobs in order,
including the jobs they enqueue, until none is left or
@code{max_jobs} were run (no limit if @code{max_jobs} is negative), and
returns their number with @code{ctx} set to NULL, or stops at the
first job which throws and returns -1 with @code{ctx} set as
above. Jobs cannot be suspended (see @code{JS_RequestSuspend()}), so
the batch does not change when a suspension can happen; a host which
must regain control regularly bounds the batch with @code{max_jobs}.

@subsection Execution timeout and interrupts

Use @code{JS_SetInterruptHandler()} to set a callback which is
regularly called by the engine when it is executing code. This
callback can be used to implement an execution timeout. It is also
called during the long BigInt operations (multiplication, division,
power, conversions from and to strings) and regular expression
matching so that they can be interrupted. Nothing can stop them
without an interrupt handler.

It is used by the command line interpreter to implement a
@code{Ctrl-C} handler.

@subsection Suspension

A running synchronous execution can be suspended in-process and
resumed later. @code{JS_RequestSuspend()}, typically called from the
interrupt handler, asks for a suspension at the next safepoint (a
backward or conditional branch reached when the interrupt counter
expires). The host call which started the execution (@code{JS_Eval()}
of a script, @code{JS_Call()}, @code{JS_CallConstructor()}...) then
returns @code{JS_EXCEPTION} and @code{JS_IsSuspended()} returns
true. @code{JS_Resume()} continues the execution and returns the
completion value of that host call, or @code{JS_EXCEPTION} if it threw
or was suspended again.

The suspension only happens if every frame of the call stack is a
normal (non generator, non async) JavaScript function, the outermost
one being the function directly called by @code{JS_Call()},
@code{JS_CallConstructor()}, @code{JS_CallConstructor2()},
@code{JS_Invoke()} or the script run by @code{JS_Eval()} or
@code{JS_EvalFunction()} with no JavaScript or native code running,
and every other one being called by the previous one through a plain
call, method call, @code{new}, spread call or tail call, possibly
through bound functions. Otherwise (getters, setters, conversions,
Proxy traps, promise resolution, callbacks of native functions, eval
code, generators, async functions, jobs, including when they are
reached from host API calls such as @code{JS_GetProperty()}) the
request stays pending until a later safepoint.

If a suspended frame cannot be resumed (stack overflow, interruption),
it and its suspended callees are terminated without running their
@code{catch} and @code{finally} clauses, the closures they created
keep working, and the error is thrown at its call site.

A C function can also suspend the JavaScript code which called it,
at the call: it returns the result of @code{JS_SuspendCall()}. The
call completes when the execution is resumed, with the value given to
@code{JS_ResumeWith()} (or throws it), possibly in another process
after a snapshot. For example, a durable host function can wait for
a result that only exists after a hibernation. The caller must call
the C function directly from a suspendable stack, otherwise
@code{JS_SuspendCall()} throws a @code{TypeError}.

@code{JS_GetProgramBranchCount(ctx)} returns the number of bytecode
branch safepoints (backward and conditional branches) executed in
@code{ctx}. The count is incremented unconditionally at every such
branch and is process- and context-local (a context restored with
@code{JS_ReadSnapshot()} starts at zero), so summing it across the
process slices of a suspended-and-restored execution gives the same
count as an uninterrupted execution. Unlike counting calls to
@code{JSInterruptHandler} (which also polls on function entry and
during suspension housekeeping, and depends on
@code{JS_INTERRUPT_COUNTER_INIT}), @code{JS_GetProgramBranchCount(ctx)}
counts only guest program branches.

@subsection Heap snapshots

@code{JS_WriteSnapshot()} serializes the complete state of a runtime:
every context, object, closure, suspended generator or async function,
the pending jobs and, when @code{JS_IsSuspended()} is true, the
suspended call stack. @code{JS_ReadSnapshot()} rebuilds that state in
a fresh runtime of the same engine build, at arbitrary addresses and in
any process. Combined with the suspension, a runtime can be hibernated
in the middle of a synchronous computation, stored, and resumed later
with @code{JS_Resume()}. A snapshot is only taken when no JavaScript
code is running on the C stack: between jobs or while suspended.

Code pointers stored in the heap (C functions, job functions, lazily
created builtins) are encoded as indexes in a registry made of the
engine builtins and of the host natives listed in
@code{JSSnapshotHost}. The registry is hashed into the snapshot and
@code{JS_ReadSnapshot()} rejects snapshots written by another engine
build or with other host natives. Host classes must be registered with
the same class IDs before reading; the opaque data of their objects is
saved and restored by the @code{save_opaque} and @code{load_opaque}
hooks. Values held by the host are passed as roots and handed back by
@code{JS_ReadSnapshot()}. The host configuration of the runtime
(interrupt handler, module loader, memory limits, promise rejection
tracker, context opaque pointers, locale callbacks) is not part of the
snapshot and must be set again.

Snapshots can be incremental. A full snapshot written with
@code{JS_SNAPSHOT_BASE} (for example once the host natives are
installed and the application script has run) becomes the base of the
runtime. A later snapshot written with @code{JS_SNAPSHOT_DELTA} only
contains the objects (and closure variables, frames...) that were
modified or created since the base, their new strings and atoms, and the
roots: it is usually a few hundred bytes to a few kilobytes. A modified
object is usually saved as a patch of its base version (adding or
setting a property costs a few bytes).
@code{JS_ReadSnapshot()} takes the base and, optionally, a delta to
apply on top of it. A runtime restored from a base has that base
attached, so its own deltas are relative to the same base, and many
runtimes can share one base. While a base is attached, the runtime keeps
every object of the base alive (they are needed to compare the heap with
the base); @code{JS_DropSnapshotBase()} releases them.

A base is reproducible: runtimes of the same engine build set up the
same way (same host natives, same code run) write the same base bytes
in any process, so a host can rebuild the base instead of storing it
and only keep the deltas, as @code{qjs --drepl} does. The state of
@code{Math.random()} is not part of a base: full snapshots and deltas
save it, and a base read without a delta gets a fresh seed.

Loaded modules are saved with their bindings and their evaluation
state, so a module graph can be hibernated in the middle of a top-level
@code{await}. The init functions of C modules must be listed in the
host natives. Modules loaded after the restore (e.g. by
@code{import()}) use the module loader of the new runtime.

Weak references whose target is already collected are restored as
dead. Objects which are only weakly reachable are freed by the restored
runtime. The time based functions (@code{Date.now()}, ...) observe the
time spent while hibernated. @code{performance.now()} does not: in a
context restored from a full snapshot or a delta, it continues from its
value when the snapshot was written, so it stays monotonic for the
restored program, and @code{performance.timeOrigin} is unchanged. Like
the @code{Math.random()} state, the time origin is not part of a base: a
base read without a delta starts a new time origin. Private symbols
(@code{JS_NewPrivateSymbol()}) and their properties are restored.

@code{JS_WriteSnapshot()} fails for contexts with the Intl or Temporal
intrinsics, for C closures (@code{JS_NewCClosure()}), for
SharedArrayBuffers whose memory may be shared with
another thread and for host-class objects with opaque data when no
@code{save_opaque} hook is given. Like bytecode, a snapshot is trusted
input: truncated or mismatched snapshots are rejected, malicious ones
are not detected.

@chapter Internals

@section Bytecode

The compiler generates bytecode directly with no intermediate
representation such as a parse tree, hence it is very fast. Several
optimizations passes are done over the generated bytecode.

A stack-based bytecode was chosen because it is simple and generates
compact code.

For each function, the maximum stack size is computed at compile time so that
no runtime stack overflow tests are needed.

A separate compressed line number table is maintained for the debug
information.

Access to closure variables is optimized and is almost as fast as local
variables.

Direct @code{eval} in strict mode is optimized.

@section Executable generation

@subsection @code{qjsc} compiler

The @code{qjsc} compiler generates C sources from Javascript files. By
default the C sources are compiled with the system compiler
(@code{gcc} or @code{clang}).

The generated C source contains the bytecode of the compiled functions
or modules. If a full complete executable is needed, it also
contains a @code{main()} function with the necessary C code to initialize the
Javascript engine and to load and execute the compiled functions and
modules.

Javascript code can be mixed with C modules.

In order to have smaller executables, specific Javascript features can
be disabled, in particular @code{eval} or the regular expressions, or
@code{-fno-unused} disables the ones the compiled code does not
reference (@code{JS_EnumCodeNames()} gives the names it uses). The
code removal relies on the linker dropping the unreferenced functions
(the library is compiled with @code{-ffunction-sections
-fdata-sections}) or on the Link Time Optimization of the system
compiler.

@subsection Binary JSON

@code{qjsc} works by compiling scripts or modules and then serializing
them to a binary format. A subset of this format (without functions or
modules) can be used as binary JSON. The @code{bjson} module gives
access to it from Javascript (see @file{tests/test_bjson.js}).

Warning: the binary JSON format may change without notice, so it
should not be used to store persistent data. The @file{test_bjson.js}
example is only used to test the binary object format functions.

Besides the JSON values (including @code{undefined}, the BigInts and
the arrays with holes), the following values can be serialized:

@itemize
@item Date, ArrayBuffer (including the resizable ones), typed arrays and
the Number, String, Boolean and BigInt objects.
@item RegExp: its source and flags are written and the regular
expression is compiled again when read. Its @code{lastIndex} is reset
to 0.
@item Map and Set: their entries in insertion order. The keys and values
are serialized recursively, so object keys, shared entries and cycles
need @code{JS_WRITE_OBJ_REFERENCE}. WeakMap and WeakSet cannot be
serialized (TypeError).
@item Symbols, as values or as property keys. The registered symbols
(@code{Symbol.for()}) and the well-known symbols are read as the same
symbols; the other symbols are read as new symbols with the same
description, a symbol written several times in an output being read
as the same symbol. The private class fields are never written.
@end itemize

The other objects (functions, Proxy, Promise, WeakRef...) raise a
TypeError. With @code{JS_WRITE_OBJ_BYTECODE}, the compiled scripts and
modules (not yet evaluated, see @code{std.evalScript()} with
@code{compile_only}) can also be serialized.

The output starts with a version byte followed by a 32 bit checksum of
the rest of the output. The checksum detects the accidental
corruptions (bit flips, truncation), not the malicious changes: the
input of @code{JS_ReadObject()} must be trusted. A checksum of
@code{0xffffffff} disables the check (hand written test inputs). An
input written before the checksum field was added is rejected with a
@code{checksum error}.

With @code{JS_WRITE_OBJ_STRIP_SOURCE}, @code{JS_WriteObject()} omits the
source code of the functions (@code{Function.prototype.toString()}
then returns a native code placeholder); with
@code{JS_WRITE_OBJ_STRIP_DEBUG}, it also omits the file names and the
line number tables. Unlike @code{JS_SetStripInfo()}, which strips at
compile time, they only change the written bytecode.
@code{JS_ReadObject2()} returns the memory of the SharedArrayBuffers
read, like @code{JS_WriteObject2()} for the written ones, so that the
host can adjust its reference counts. These are the pointers returned
by the @code{sab_alloc} function set with
@code{JS_SetSharedArrayBufferFunctions()}.

@section Runtime

@subsection Strings

Strings are stored either as an 8 bit or a 16 bit array of
characters. Hence random access to characters is always fast.

The C API provides functions to convert Javascript Strings to C UTF-8 encoded
strings. The most common case where the Javascript string contains
only ASCII characters involves no copying.

@subsection Objects

The object shapes (object prototype, property names and flags) are shared
between objects to save memory.

Arrays with no holes (except at the end of the array) are optimized.

TypedArray accesses are optimized.

@subsection Atoms

Object property names and some strings are stored as Atoms (unique
strings) to save memory and allow fast comparison. Atoms are
represented as a 32 bit integer. Half of the atom range is reserved for
immediate integer literals from @math{0} to @math{2^{31}-1}.

@subsection Numbers

Numbers are represented either as 32-bit signed integers or 64-bit IEEE-754
floating point values. Most operations have fast paths for the 32-bit
integer case.

@subsection Garbage collection

Reference counting is used to free objects automatically and
deterministically. A separate cycle removal pass is done when the allocated
memory becomes too large. The cycle removal algorithm only uses the
reference counts and the object content, so no explicit garbage
collection roots need to be manipulated in the C code.

@subsection JSValue

It is a Javascript value which can be a primitive type (such as
Number, String, ...) or an Object. NaN boxing is used in the 32-bit version
to store 64-bit floating point numbers. The representation is
optimized so that 32-bit integers and reference counted values can be
efficiently tested.

In 64-bit code, JSValue are 128-bit large and no NaN boxing is used. The
rationale is that in 64-bit code memory usage is less critical.

In both cases (32 or 64 bits), JSValue exactly fits two CPU registers,
so it can be efficiently returned by C functions.

@subsection Function call

The engine is optimized so that function calls are fast. The system
stack holds the Javascript parameters and local variables.

@section RegExp

A specific regular expression engine was developed. It is both small
and efficient and supports all the ES2025 features including the
Unicode properties. As the Javascript compiler, it directly generates
bytecode without a parse tree.

Backtracking with an explicit stack is used so that there is no
recursion on the system stack. Simple quantifiers are specifically
optimized to avoid recursions.

The full regexp library weights about 15 KiB (x86 code), excluding the
Unicode library.

@section Unicode

A specific Unicode library was developed so that there is no
dependency on an external large Unicode library such as ICU. All the
Unicode tables are compressed while keeping a reasonable access
speed.

The library supports case conversion, Unicode normalization, Unicode
script queries, Unicode general category queries and all Unicode
binary properties.

The full Unicode library weights about 45 KiB (x86 code).

@section BigInt

BigInts are represented using binary two's complement notation. An
additional short bigint value is used to optimize the performance on
small BigInt values.

